.TH DUP 2
.SH NAME
dup \- duplicate an open file descriptor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "dup :: proc(old: int, new: int = -1) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dup
makes
.I new
a second descriptor for the file
.I old
already names, and answers the descriptor used.
.PP
If
.I new
is \-1, the lowest free descriptor is chosen.
Otherwise
.I new
names the descriptor to use, closing whatever it held first.
.PP
A process holds at most thirty-two descriptors, numbered 0 to 31; unlike
Plan 9, there is no growing the table by naming a higher
.IR new .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR pipe (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers the descriptor used on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.B EBADF
is
.I old
not open, or
.I new
naming a number the descriptor table does not reach.
